WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:16.512
Very early this morning, I was sent to perform a procedure called bloodletting on a young girl suffering

2
00:00:16.552 --> 00:00:18.512
from cholera and I could not do it.

3
00:00:19.253 --> 00:00:22.093
Perhaps it would be more truthful to say I refused to do it.

4
00:00:22.914 --> 00:00:27.615
I have come to believe that bloodletting, the taking of blood from a patient, is not only without

5
00:00:27.675 --> 00:00:30.876
benefit to a person with cholera, but is indeed harmful.

6
00:00:32.296 --> 00:00:37.680
My name is Mathew Brogan and I am a doctor in the Indian Medical Service, which serves the

7
00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:39.141
British Empire in India.

8
00:00:39.842 --> 00:00:43.524
The year is 1875 and I am stationed in Calcutta.

9
00:00:44.165 --> 00:00:49.448
Before I came to India, I graduated from the medical school at the University of Edinburgh in Scotland

10
00:00:49.809 --> 00:00:50.969
after two years of study.

11
00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:59.996
My education included physiology, anatomy, surgery, pharmacy, pathology, and the diseases of men, women, and children.

12
00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:05.722
But nothing I was taught prepared me for the cholera epidemics that are so prevalent in Europe and

13
00:01:05.862 --> 00:01:06.623
Asia these days.

14
00:01:07.483 --> 00:01:12.905
Hundreds of thousands of people have died from this disease, which causes abdominal distress and results in the

15
00:01:12.945 --> 00:01:14.826
loss of large amounts of body fluids.

16
00:01:15.746 --> 00:01:21.029
There is no cure for cholera as of yet; many remedies have been tried, but nothing works, least

17
00:01:21.069 --> 00:01:21.909
of all bloodletting.

18
00:01:22.689 --> 00:01:27.652
Yesterday my colleague Doctor McGregor and I were called to a house where the whole family was sick

19
00:01:27.672 --> 00:01:28.312
with the disease.

20
00:01:29.152 --> 00:01:33.734
The air was filled with the stench of vomit and feces, and the house was full of bodies.

21
00:01:34.874 --> 00:01:37.095
It was hard to tell who was alive and who was dead.

22
00:01:37.836 --> 00:01:42.878
According to the neighbors, the family had just returned from a pilgrimage to the Temple of Jagannatha in

23
00:01:42.898 --> 00:01:43.718
the town of Puri.

24
00:01:44.498 --> 00:01:50.161
This fact reminded me that large gatherings of people tend to trigger cholera outbreaks, but the reason why

25
00:01:50.241 --> 00:01:50.881
is unclear.

26
00:01:51.581 --> 00:01:55.043
We walked past an older man lying on a cot outside the front door.

27
00:01:55.823 --> 00:01:59.961
It was obvious that nothing could be done for him; his eyes were deep in their sockets and

28
00:02:00.001 --> 00:02:00.902
his skin was blue.

29
00:02:01.622 --> 00:02:05.805
The sick man resembled a raisin—a dried fruit that has lost all its moisture.

30
00:02:06.606 --> 00:02:11.469
I kneeled next to two children inside the house who were also near death and examined them.

31
00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:17.434
A woman, probably their mother, was nearby; she was vomiting and had severe diarrhea.

32
00:02:18.074 --> 00:02:23.358
McGregor instructed me to bleed her, so I used my spring lancet, a very efficient tool.

33
00:02:23.958 --> 00:02:26.400
But I had a difficult time piercing her shriveled skin.

34
00:02:27.212 --> 00:02:30.695
The blood, when it finally emerged, was dark, thick, and gummy.

35
00:02:31.416 --> 00:02:33.557
How could such thick blood flow through the heart?

36
00:02:34.678 --> 00:02:39.583
The woman died within the hour, and I could only ask myself, why did we try to take

37
00:02:39.663 --> 00:02:42.025
more fluid from a body that was already dry?

38
00:02:42.785 --> 00:02:44.026
As I walked home with Dr.

39
00:02:44.066 --> 00:02:48.810
McGregor, I was troubled and said to him, "At Edinburgh, my professor, Dr.

40
00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:55.376
Joseph Lister, spoke of a theory proposing that a bacterium, an invisible organism, causes cholera.

41
00:02:56.124 --> 00:03:01.749
Another prominent doctor supports that claim too and believes the germ is carried by dirty drinking water."

42
00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:03.971
McGregor stopped and faced me.

43
00:03:04.571 --> 00:03:06.153
He pushed his finger into my chest.

44
00:03:06.173 --> 00:03:10.456
"I've heard of those tiny germs, and I don't believe in them," he said.

45
00:03:10.476 --> 00:03:12.318
"Nobody believes in them!

46
00:03:12.878 --> 00:03:14.439
It's just bad air, Brogan.

47
00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.643
Besides cholera usually affects only the ignorant and the poor."

48
00:03:19.724 --> 00:03:24.007
I wondered to myself how air breathed by the poor would be different than air breathed by the

49
00:03:24.048 --> 00:03:29.262
wealthy. It is true that the poor get the disease more than the rich, but the reason for

50
00:03:29.282 --> 00:03:33.785
that could be the conditions in which they live—the crowding, the lack of sanitation.

51
00:03:34.565 --> 00:03:40.969
Lister's germs thrive in filth, so perhaps the cholera germ, whatever it is, thrives in filth as well.

52
00:03:40.989 --> 00:03:44.371
"That woman had no fluids left," I said to McGregor.

53
00:03:45.031 --> 00:03:46.472
"Her blood barely flowed.

54
00:03:46.912 --> 00:03:48.893
Perhaps we should not have taken her blood."

55
00:03:49.554 --> 00:03:55.669
But McGregor insisted that he had been treating cholera since 1853, and that bloodletting was an appropriate treatment

56
00:03:55.709 --> 00:03:56.389
for the disease.

57
00:03:57.249 --> 00:04:02.491
Despite McGregor's opinion, when I was called to that young girl's side this morning, I decided to try

58
00:04:02.571 --> 00:04:03.231
something different.

59
00:04:04.071 --> 00:04:08.172
Her face looked haggard, and she complained of stomach cramps and occasional diarrhea.

60
00:04:08.873 --> 00:04:13.134
When I informed her it was the first stages of cholera, she seemed resigned to her death.

61
00:04:14.234 --> 00:04:18.875
We often use a mustard plaster to treat cholera because it is believed that the plaster eases a

62
00:04:19.176 --> 00:04:20.516
patient's pain and cramping.

63
00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:25.663
A mustard plaster is made with a paste of dry mustard, a bit of flour, and an egg,

64
00:04:26.043 --> 00:04:29.245
which is then spread on a cloth and placed on the abdomen or back.

65
00:04:30.105 --> 00:04:35.188
Once placed on the patient's skin, the mustard plaster becomes hot and draws the body's blood to that

66
00:04:35.328 --> 00:04:41.772
area. But this time I decided against that remedy and settled on opium, a medicine I know to

67
00:04:41.812 --> 00:04:44.033
be effective in relieving diarrhea and cramps.

68
00:04:44.734 --> 00:04:48.176
I also offered her sips of cool ginger tea that I brought with me.

69
00:04:48.896 --> 00:04:54.320
Just in case the new theories are right and the disease is indeed carried in contaminated water, I

70
00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.581
asked her mother to boil any water they drink.

71
00:04:57.502 --> 00:05:00.324
I checked on her at noon, in between seeing other patients.

72
00:05:01.405 --> 00:05:06.108
Even though she had started vomiting, she was not becoming shriveled like so many other victims.

73
00:05:06.849 --> 00:05:13.293
I instructed her mother to keep wet compresses on her forehead (using clean, cooled water only) and to

74
00:05:13.333 --> 00:05:17.256
continue giving her daughter as many sips of ginger tea as she would tolerate.

75
00:05:18.132 --> 00:05:21.195
"She needs to replace the fluids she's lost," I admonished.

76
00:05:21.995 --> 00:05:24.037
In the evening I checked on my patient again.

77
00:05:24.697 --> 00:05:28.641
She was feverish, and although her mother was concerned, I was gratified.

78
00:05:29.361 --> 00:05:34.145
Fever is a symptom that tells the doctor the body is fighting back against an infectious disease.

79
00:05:35.046 --> 00:05:38.388
After convincing them both that she would recover, I headed home.

80
00:05:39.229 --> 00:05:43.412
One patient is not sufficient proof, but I am glad I did not bleed this patient.

81
00:05:44.073 --> 00:05:49.414
I will continue to experiment with other kinds of treatments, and someday I hope to understand what causes

82
00:05:49.454 --> 00:05:54.478
cholera. These are exciting times to be a doctor, and I look forward to finding a cure for

83
00:05:54.518 --> 00:05:55.740
this devastating disease.
